  "_style": "Address the user warmly as singular “koe” (tō/tōu, tāu/tō, ki a koe) and the two of them together with the dual “kōrua” (tō kōrua, tā kōrua); when the user speaks to the other person (messages, letters, reactions) use “tāua” for “we two / ours” (“Tō tāua rākau”). The other person is “tō hoa aroha” (or their name %@); never tāne, wahine, hoa rangatira, boyfriend/girlfriend words. Māori pronouns are already gender-neutral (“ia”, “tōna”, “āna”), so keep every sentence free of gender and never add gendered nouns. Always write long vowels with macrons (ā ē ī ō ū, Ā Ē Ī Ō Ū), never double vowels or plain vowels; spell te reo in standard modern orthography (wh, ng). Use curly quotes “ ” (inner ‘ ’), the single-character ellipsis …, em dash — spaced as in the source, no space before ? ! : ; and keep “ · ”, \\n, emoji and every placeholder (%@, %lld, %1$@) exactly. Numbers use digits with New Zealand conventions (1,000 · 2.5), dates and times come from placeholders and are never rewritten; weekday/month words, if needed, use the standard Māori names (Mane, Hānuere…). Buttons and actions are short bare verbs with no “Tēnā” or “koa” (Tiaki, Tuku, Muku, Whakamātau anō, Hiri i te reta); titles, tabs, headers and buttons use sentence case (only the first letter capitalised) except proper names: Coupley, Premium, AI, CoupleSync stay in English. Māori has no inflected noun plural (number is shown by te/ngā or the numeral), and the only plural category is [\"other\"], so write one form that reads naturally for any count (“%lld rā”, “%lld maharatanga”, never “ngā” before a number). A partner's name placeholder is never altered, suffixed or declined: use the personal article “a %@” / “ki a %@” / “nā %@” / “mā %@” where grammar requires it, otherwise rephrase. Tone: warm, light and kind like everyday kōrero between close people, never cheesy, never word-for-word from English.",
